<main>
  <div class="eyebrow">BloodTest.in Health Guide</div>
  <h1>What You Need to Know About LDL Cholesterol</h1>
  <p class="intro">LDL cholesterol is one of the most important measurements in a lipid profile. Learn what LDL means, how high LDL can affect cardiovascular health, how it is tested, and what you can do to manage your levels.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <strong>Quick answer:</strong> LDL stands for low-density lipoprotein. It carries cholesterol through the bloodstream, but excessive LDL can contribute to plaque buildup in arteries and increase cardiovascular risk.
  </div>

  <h2>What Is LDL Cholesterol?</h2>
  <p>LDL, or low-density lipoprotein, transports cholesterol through the bloodstream. Cholesterol is necessary for several normal body functions, but too much LDL can contribute to atherosclerosis, in which plaque builds up inside the arteries.</p>

  <h2>Why Is High LDL a Concern?</h2>
  <p>High LDL usually does not cause noticeable symptoms. Over time, however, excess LDL can contribute to plaque formation and may increase the risk of coronary artery disease, heart attack and stroke.</p>

  <h2>What Is a Healthy LDL Level?</h2>
  <p>For many adults, an LDL level below 100 mg/dL is generally considered desirable. However, the appropriate LDL goal depends on a person's overall cardiovascular risk and medical history.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>LDL level</th><th>General interpretation</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Less than 100 mg/dL</td><td>Generally desirable</td></tr>
      <tr><td>100–129 mg/dL</td><td>Near optimal for some people</td></tr>
      <tr><td>130–159 mg/dL</td><td>Borderline high</td></tr>
      <tr><td>160–189 mg/dL</td><td>High</td></tr>
      <tr><td>190 mg/dL or higher</td><td>Very high</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What Causes High LDL Cholesterol?</h2>
  <p>High LDL can be influenced by diet, excess weight, physical inactivity, genetics and certain medical conditions or medicines. Familial hypercholesterolemia is an inherited condition that can cause particularly high cholesterol.</p>

  <h2>How Is LDL Cholesterol Tested?</h2>
  <p>LDL cholesterol is commonly included in a lipid profile or lipid panel. The test may also report total cholesterol, HDL cholesterol, triglycerides and other lipid measurements. Whether fasting is required depends on the testing circumstances and your healthcare provider's instructions.</p>

  <h2>How Can You Lower LDL?</h2>
  <p>Lifestyle measures can help improve cholesterol levels. Depending on your individual risk, a healthcare professional may also recommend cholesterol-lowering medicines.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Choose more vegetables, fruits, whole grains and legumes.</li>
    <li>Reduce foods high in saturated fat.</li>
    <li>Stay physically active.</li>
    <li>Maintain a healthy weight.</li>
    <li>Avoid smoking.</li>
    <li>Follow medical advice about cholesterol-lowering treatment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When Should You Speak With a Doctor?</h2>
  <p>If your LDL remains elevated, is very high, or you have diabetes, cardiovascular disease, a strong family history of high cholesterol or other cardiovascular risk factors, discuss your results with a qualified healthcare professional.</p>

